  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 1122 AM MST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nA relatively weak Pacific low is still slated to move inland,\ncrossing the Great Basin Tuesday night and moving into CO on\nWednesday. This low still looks to carry scant showers within its\ncore, but no moisture tap will sync up with it while it remains\nvoid of strong dynamics aloft. Therefore, just a smattering of\nlight showers is in the forecast along the NM-CO border for\nWednesday with temperatures rising a few degrees. Winds would\nincrease a bit, but most areas will just stay in the breezy\ncategory with a few west southwesterly gusts of 30 to 35 mph \nalong the central mountain chain into the northeast highlands.\n\nInto Thursday, the CO low will drop southeastward into the\nsouthern plains with cooler air filtering into NM on the backside.\nCold air advection will be modest with daytime highs running\nslightly below normal. The backdoor segment of this front would \nlead to strong northerly winds in the far eastern plains.\nCurrently gusts are forecast to reach about 30-35 mph in the far\neastern plains Thursday, but these might need to trend upward on\nfuture shifts. \n\nThe next upstream feature will be a baggy, weak trough that will\nstretch from the central Rockies into the southwestern states on\nFriday. This will keep winds aloft light over NM with low\nprospects for precipitation due to a lack of forcing. This feature\nwill slide southeastward into Saturday, and a ridge of high\npressure will then build over NM and surrounding areas Sunday into\nMonday of next week. There are hints of a weak upstream cut-off \nlow developing over or just west of the Baja on deterministic \nmodel runs by next Monday, but subsequently this feature does not\nlook to ingest much subtropical moisture or pose large impacts to\nNM at this time.",
